# React Frontend Guidelines

## Component Structure

- Use functional React components.
- Keep components focused and maintainable.
- Reuse existing components before creating new ones.
- Avoid unnecessary component duplication.
- Keep reusable UI logic in shared components or utilities.
- Do not introduce unnecessary state or effects.
- Follow the existing project architecture and naming conventions.

## Responsive Design

The portfolio must work well across:

- Desktop
- Laptop
- Tablet
- Mobile

Never design only for the current desktop viewport.

When modifying a section, check how it behaves at different viewport widths.

Avoid fixed widths that can cause:

- Content overflow
- Overlapping sections
- Text collisions
- Excessive empty space
- Horizontally scrolling pages

Prefer responsive Tailwind classes and flexible layouts.

Use:

- `w-full`
- `max-w-*`
- `mx-auto`
- `px-*`
- `gap-*`
- responsive breakpoints
- CSS Grid
- Flexbox

instead of unnecessary fixed pixel dimensions.

## Layout Spacing

Maintain consistent spacing between:

- Section headings
- Subheadings
- Cards
- Buttons
- Navigation elements
- Content blocks

Do not allow unrelated elements to visually touch each other.

Avoid excessive padding or margins that make sections unnecessarily tall.

Use consistent spacing scales instead of arbitrary values.

## Cards

Cards should have enough internal spacing so that:

- Titles do not touch borders.
- Descriptions have comfortable line height.
- Technology badges have adequate spacing.
- Buttons and links do not overlap content.
- Card content remains readable at smaller widths.

Cards should grow naturally with their content.

Do not force fixed heights unless the design specifically requires them.

When multiple cards are displayed in a grid:

- Keep consistent gaps.
- Allow cards to resize naturally.
- Ensure text wraps correctly.
- Prevent buttons or links from overlapping card content.

## Typography

Maintain a clear visual hierarchy.

Use appropriate differences between:

- Section labels
- Headings
- Subheadings
- Body text
- Metadata
- Buttons
- Tags

Do not use unnecessarily large text that causes wrapping or layout problems.

Long titles and descriptions must be allowed to wrap naturally.

Avoid:

- `white-space: nowrap` unless specifically required.
- Fixed-width text containers that cause overflow.
- Extremely large headings on small screens.

Use responsive typography where appropriate.

